> It is difficult to measure how many unnecessary forks were created during a normal run because of this detail, so I'm not going to do it.

Nevermind, I just thought of a simple way to get an upper bound on `master`:

```c++
	_FORCE_INLINE_ Iterator begin() {
		if (((Vector<T> *)this)->refcount() > 1) {
			std::cout << "Fork " << ((Vector<T> *)this)->size() << " x " << sizeof(T) << std::endl;
		}
		return Iterator(((Vector<T> *)this)->ptrw());
	}
	_FORCE_INLINE_ Iterator end() {
		if (((Vector<T> *)this)->refcount() > 1) {
			std::cout << "Fork " << ((Vector<T> *)this)->size() << " x " << sizeof(T) << std::endl;
		}

		return Iterator(((Vector<T> *)this)->ptrw() + ((Vector<T> *)this)->size());
	}
```

I get get the following results, when loading into an empty project:
```
Fork 1 x 72 (24 times)
Fork 2 x 72 (6 times)
```

So, it seems like this change is definitely not critical to at least the launch process. I'm guessing this is because most in-engine code hands out references rather than CoW copies, so probably the biggest beneficiary, if anything, may be code taking in vectors from GDScript. Though I'd have to search for an example where this is actually impactful.
Anyway, I'd still argue it would be good to merge it, because conceptually it's bad not to be able to iterate a vector you observe without needing a fork. But it's probably not urgent to do so.
